ed data for search engines. Since I don't have images available, I'll skip them or use CSS decorations instead. For the content, I'm framing this as a free sample with the Prologue and first three chapters in full, then a \"Continue Reading\" section with purchase links for Kindle, Paperback, and Audiobook through affiliate programs, plus a newsletter signup and cross-promotion of other Oz books to drive revenue.\n\nFor the Amazon links, I'll use placeholder affiliate tags rather than inventing ASINs, and I need to handle paragraph breaks carefully when splitting the text—particularly around dialogue where quotes can appear mid-paragraph versus starting a new one.\n\nI'm refining the regex pattern to split on whitespace between sentence-ending punctuation and opening quotes, which will catch most paragraph breaks while accepting some false splits within dialogue as acceptable trade-offs.\n\nNow I need to generate the full HTML output—roughly 45K characters—and embed it as a single line in my JSON response. Rather than building it in Python and reprinting it (which would waste tokens), I should compose the HTML directly in my final answer by pulling the text I have, though I'll need to reconstruct chapters 2 and 3 from what I've seen so far. I'm settling on including the Prologue through Chapter 3 to end at a strong cliffhanger when the Patchwork Girl comes alive, which gives me roughly 31K characters for the sample. Now I need to retrieve the remaining portions of chapters 2 and 3 that I don't have in context yet to work with the full text.I've got the first three chapters and the prologue loaded, with Chapter 3ending nicely as the Glass Cat arrives. Now I'm building out the HTML page with proper structure — setting up the head section with character encoding, viewport settings, SEO metadata, Open Graph tags for social sharing, and JSON-LD schema markup to identify this as a book. I'm styling it with readable serif typography, constraining the width for comfortable 