sing proper formatting and typography. I've also mapped out the key improvements I want to implement: a responsive design with a sticky header call-to-action, a hero section with clear value proposition, an interactive table of contents, a gated sample that funnels toward purchase options across different formats, newsletter signup, navigation between other Oz books, SEO metadata with structured data, and trust signals like the public domain note and Gutenberg link.\n\nWriting the final JSON structure... Closing the JSON-LD script tag, then I'll finalize the metadata and structure details. I'm setting up chapter IDs for the table of contents, keeping entries 4-28 compact in a two-column layout with lock indicators pointing to the full book. I'll add a subtle drop cap styling to the first paragraph of each chapter for visual polish.\n\nFor navigation, I'm linking to the previous and next books in the series using root-relative paths to handle any server setup. I'll insert a small promotional banner between chapters 2 and 3 encouraging readers to get the illustrated edition. Throughout the dialogue sections, I'm converting double dashes to em-dashes and preserving the original paragraph breaks and spacing as transcribed.Now I'm putting together the final JSON output with the enhanced HTML, making sure to use single quotes for the HTML attributes and proper curly quotes in any text content.","reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The web_fetch gave me markdown-converted content instead of raw HTML, so I need to find another way to access the original HTML structure. Since I don't have direct raw HTML access, I'll need to work with what I have and reconstruct the page layout from the converted content.I'm looking at a 312KB public domain book text and need to improve its HTML for better conversion rates, but the full content is too large to include in a single response since it would consume too many tokens. I need to figure out how to handle returning co