 of dialogue with double quotes that would need escaping anyway. I think I'll convert the dialogue quotes to curly quotes and apostrophes instead—that way I avoid most escaping and get better typography as a bonus.\n\nFor the HTML structure itself, I'll use single quotes on attributes to keep things cleaner, and I'll handle the JSON-LD carefully by escaping its double quotes. I'll also convert the em-dashes to proper typographic dashes while I'm at it.\n\nWriting the HTML structure... Writing the page structure...\n\nI'm wrapping up the improvements list—conversion-focused hero with clear CTAs, strategically placed buy boxes with affiliate links, email capture at key moments to build the audience, and linked chapter navigation.\n\nThen I'm handling the structural and technical side: semantic markup with readable typography to keep readers engaged, preserving the original book text verbatim in chapters 2–24, adding SEO fundamentals like schema markup and meta tags, making it responsive for mobile, adding prev/next navigation and cross-links between books, a reading progress bar and back-to-top button, plus accessibility features like skip links and semantic landmarks.\n\nNow I'm generating the final HTML output. the other inhabitants of this magical land. The preface goes on to explain how difficult it was to get Trot and Cap'n Bill safely to Oz, crediting the Scarecrow with saving them from danger, and notes that Dorothy has promised more adventures with Button-Bright and the three girls will come in future books.\n\nThe author then expresses gratitude to readers for their encouragement, promising to continue writing as long as they're interested, and signs off as the Royal Historian of Oz from his home in Hollywood, California in 1915. He dedicates the book to the uplifters of Los Angeles, praising their efforts to improve humanity through kindness and fellowship.\n\nNow I'm transcribing the opening chapter, converting the punctuation to match the style—replacing s